24046d5f-b4a2-436f-8029-4f111b6c1192	beer_syria_2500_bce	Beer (Syria, 2500 BCE)	Beer	Grain	35.798000	36.798000	\N	\N	2500 BCE	-2500	beer_ancient_egypt_3500_bce	Documentation found in the Elba Tablets. Brewers were typically priestesses.	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	f574a2d9-40d9-49d0-86dd-c0f1866ad7ee	2026-02-03 16:13:48.178567+00	2026-02-03 16:22:29.008942+00	f	approved	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
d12b8ce8-55db-40c7-b0ed-6749c1174889	ricewine_china_7000_bce	Rice Wine (China, 7000 BCE)	Wine	Grain	33.612900	113.667400	\N	\N	7000 BCE	-7000	\N	The Ancient Chinese were producing fermented drinks that were similar to a wine. They weren't technically a wine, as they were made from rice, honey, and fruit. Scientists concluded this from examining residues of the ancient shards of pottery from a Stage Age site in north China.	\N	\N	https://phys.org/news/2004-12-chinese-people-wine-years.html#:~:text=Physical%20evidence%20have%20recently%20confirmed,the%20oldest%20playable%20instruments%2D%2D	\N	\N	\N	f574a2d9-40d9-49d0-86dd-c0f1866ad7ee	2026-02-03 16:13:48.178567+00	2026-02-03 16:27:05.687127+00	f	approved	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
72072f28-ba77-473a-91a0-1c9c0923e5f5	wine_armenia_4100_bce	Wine (Armenia, 4100 BCE)	Wine	Grape	39.726100	45.213600	\N	\N	4100 BCE	-4100	\N	Armenia is one of the world’s oldest known centers of winemaking and viticulture, and offers some of the most compelling archaeological evidence for the origins of wine. The most significant discovery is the Areni-1 Cave complex, where archaeologists unearthed the oldest known winery, dating to approximately 4,100 BCE.	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	f574a2d9-40d9-49d0-86dd-c0f1866ad7ee	2026-02-03 16:13:48.178567+00	2026-02-03 16:27:25.823681+00	f	approved	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
61f6fb13-7252-40ca-983b-ebf6edb15847	wine_canaan_6500_bce	Wine (Canaan, 6500 BCE)	Wine	Grape	32.766600	35.333300	\N	\N	6500 BCE	-6500	\N	Winemaking was believed to have been acquired as an industry in Ancient Egypt from trade with what used to be Canaan in the Levant region.	\N	\N	https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Egyptian_wine	\N	\N	\N	f574a2d9-40d9-49d0-86dd-c0f1866ad7ee	2026-02-03 16:13:48.178567+00	2026-02-03 16:27:46.745198+00	f	approved	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
670a9668-5c07-42d5-a0a6-929c9df12ae9	wine_egypt_2600_bce	Wine (Egypt, 2600 BCE)	Wine	Grape	27.037600	29.964300	\N	\N	2600 BCE	-2600	wine_canaan_6500_bce	\N	\N	\N	J. Robinson (ed) "The Oxford Companion to Wine" Third Edition pp. 326–329 Oxford University Press 2006 ISBN 0-19-860990-6	\N	\N	\N	f574a2d9-40d9-49d0-86dd-c0f1866ad7ee	2026-02-03 16:13:48.178567+00	2026-02-03 16:28:34.148755+00	f	approved	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
b45ddf98-014f-46e9-897e-c9b9c5a27b6a	wine_georgia_6000_bce	Wine (Georgia, 6000 BCE)	Wine	Grape	41.318200	44.685100	\N	\N	6000 BCE	-6000	\N	Evidence of the world's earliest winemaking was uncovered by a series of excavations in the modern-day nation of Georgia. Fragments of pottery from two neolithic villages were examined and revealed traces of tartaric acid, which is found in grapes in large quantities. The teams of excavators note that while it is possible that the containers simply stored grapes or juice, anything of the sort would have degraded without a trace, or fermented within a matter of days.	\N	\N	https://www.theguardian.com/science/2017/nov/13/evidence-of-worlds-earliest-winemaking-uncovered-by-archaeologists	\N	\N	\N	f574a2d9-40d9-49d0-86dd-c0f1866ad7ee	2026-02-03 16:13:48.178567+00	2026-02-03 16:29:22.516789+00	f	approved	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
59cc932b-40c9-4564-acc4-7d99f553d2fb	wine_greece_1200_bce	Wine (Greece, 1200 BCE)	Wine	Grape	39.405500	22.588500	\N	\N	1200 BCE	-1200	wine_egypt_2600_bce	Through trading, Greece had learned from Ancient Egypt about winemaking techniques.	\N	\N	J. Robinson (ed) "The Oxford Companion to Wine" Third Edition pp. 326–329 Oxford University Press 2006 ISBN 0-19-860990-6	\N	\N	\N	f574a2d9-40d9-49d0-86dd-c0f1866ad7ee	2026-02-03 16:13:48.178567+00	2026-02-03 16:29:53.82635+00	f	approved	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
bbc9de94-56ad-47a5-8e3e-64fb6216214d	wine_greece_6500_bce	Wine (Greece, 6500 BCE)	Wine	Grape	39.405500	22.588500	\N	\N	6500 BCE	-6500	\N	Viticulture had started around this time. While not recorded for winemaking, it was a key stepping stone in th winemaking process.	\N	\N	J. Robinson (ed) "The Oxford Companion to Wine" Third Edition pp. 326–329 Oxford University Press 2006 ISBN 0-19-860990-6	\N	\N	\N	f574a2d9-40d9-49d0-86dd-c0f1866ad7ee	2026-02-03 16:13:48.178567+00	2026-02-03 16:30:11.78629+00	f	approved	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
76a5082d-ff21-4a9b-a629-bbd9809ba0b0	beer_ancient_egypt_3400_bce	Beer (Ancient Egypt, 3400 BCE)	Beer	Grain	26.820600	30.802500	\N	\N	3400 BCE	-3400	\N	"Similar styles of brew were common in ancient Egypt, whose writings and artifacts likewise provide a detailed record of what its inhabitants drank and hint at some of their reasons for doing so. The story of Egyptian drinking begins in the city of Hierakonpolis, whose ruins contain the remains of the world's oldest brewery, dating to circa 3400 c. It was capable of producing up to three hundred gallons per day of a Sumerian-style brew. Heirakonpolis was also the site of a thriving pottery industry whose principal products were beer jugs and cups, the shards of which litter the ruins of the city. The sheer abundance of such relics and the relative scale of the brewing operations imply that beer was a vital part of the diet of the people who lived there."	\N	\N	Drink - Iain Gately Page 5/6	\N	\N	\N	f574a2d9-40d9-49d0-86dd-c0f1866ad7ee	2026-02-03 16:13:48.178567+00	2026-02-03 16:21:07.213366+00	f	approved	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
7b809291-0432-49d8-8186-85bfc702d22a	beer_fertile_crescent_2900_bce	Beer (Fertile Crescent, 2900 BCE)	Beer	Grain	34.806800	40.928600	\N	\N	2900 BCE	-2900	\N	"The first proof that beer was being brewed in the region derives from the residues of an alcoholic barley brew found in a pottery vessel at Godin Tepe, in the Zagros Mountains of Iran, and dating to 3100-2900 Bc. As is the case with the wine of Haji Firuz Tepe, whether this ur-beer was made to stimulate or simply as a kind of food remains a mystery."	\N	\N	Drink - Iain Gately Page 3	\N	\N	\N	f574a2d9-40d9-49d0-86dd-c0f1866ad7ee	2026-02-03 16:13:48.178567+00	2026-02-03 16:21:40.413585+00	f	approved	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
012cbaeb-9e22-4f54-ab13-de62fb5bfbfc	wine_fertile_crescent_5000_bce	Wine (Fertile Crescent, 5000 BCE)	Wine	Grape	34.806800	40.928600	\N	\N	5000 BCE	-5000	\N	"Proof that people were cultivating plants to manufacture alcohol first appears in the so-called Fertile Crescent, a geographical area curving between the Mediterranean and the Persian Gulf. The analysis of a yellow residue found on the inside of a jar at a Neolithic settlement in Haji Firuz Tepe (Iran), dating to 5400-5000 Bc, revealed that the jar had once held wine. The residence in which it was discovered had six such jars, and other houses in the same hillside village also had similar vessels, in comparable quantities. The amount of wine each household might have produced suggests that it was a small but significant part of their diet. In such communities, winemaking was the best technology they had for storing highly perishable grapes, although whether the resulting fluid was intended for intoxication as well as nourishment is unknown. It is likely that the inhabitants of the Fertile Crescent were also making mead from honey and beer from their surplus grain in the same era."	\N	\N	Drink - Iain Gately Page 3	\N	\N	\N	f574a2d9-40d9-49d0-86dd-c0f1866ad7ee	2026-02-03 16:13:48.178567+00	2026-02-03 16:29:01.674901+00	f	approved	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
7130bd07-2bd1-4eed-ac1e-33f10aa9decc	rice wine_jiahu_6600_bce	Rice Wine (Jiahu, 6600 BCE)	Wine	Grain	33.612900	113.667300	\N	\N	6600 BCE	-6600	ricewine_china_7000_bce	"Definite evidence of the preparation of alcoholic drinks first appears around 8000 BC after humanity took up agriculture and established sedentary communities. The earliest proof that they were converting some of their produce into brews derives from the chemical analysis of  the residues found inside pottery jars discovered in a grave in Jiahu, in northern China, and dating to 7000-6600 BC. These clay vessels coincidentally the most ancient of their kind, contained a fermented drink made with rice, honey, grapes, and hawthorn berries."	\N	\N	Drink - Iain Gately Page 2	\N	\N	\N	f574a2d9-40d9-49d0-86dd-c0f1866ad7ee	2026-02-03 16:13:48.178567+00	2026-02-03 16:26:50.815113+00	f	approved	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
6f0bbe4c-f72f-402f-8d70-b7d973d213ce	wine_caucasus_region_5800_bce	Wine (Caucasus Region, 5800 BCE)	Wine	Grape	42.154000	44.071600	\N	\N	5800 BCE	-5800	\N	The earliest evidence of grape winemaking traces back over 8,000 years to the South Caucasus, a region widely recognized as a cradle of grape domestication and early wine culture (includes present-day Armenia, Georgia, and Azerbaijan). Rather than a single point of origin, winemaking appears to have developed across several communities in this region, with archaeological sites such as Areni-1 (Armenia) and Shulaveri (Georgia) showing independent but contemporaneous traditions. From here, winemaking spread throughout the ancient Near East and Mediterranean world, eventually reaching every inhabited continent.	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	f574a2d9-40d9-49d0-86dd-c0f1866ad7ee	2026-02-03 16:13:48.178567+00	2026-02-03 16:28:12.035229+00	f	approved	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
715132d0-235e-4042-869b-c4826798ac5e	green-chartreuse	Green Chartreuse 	Liqueur	Sugar	45.363333	5.793611	Chartreuse Mountains, north of Grenoble	France	15th Century	1605	sugar-cane-spirit	The liqueur has been made by Carthusian monks since 1737	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	81c2560c-576c-4ac3-98b9-8e79a4843e3e	81c2560c-576c-4ac3-98b9-8e79a4843e3e	2026-02-04 02:32:12.330639+00	2026-02-04 02:50:45.206647+00	f	approved	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
f0cabb0a-ce7a-4778-b6e6-0b42cd2a556f	sugar-cane-spirit	Sugar Cane Spirit	Spirit	Sugar	23.995000	67.435000	\N	\N	327 BCE	-327	\N	First recorded mention of sugar cane juice is from Alexander the Great’s general Nearchus, during his conquest of the Indus river region: “A reed in India brings forth honey without the help of bees, from which an intoxicating drink is made though the plant bears no fruit.”	\N	\N	https://www.spiritsanddistilling.com/dictionary/acref-9780199311132-e-695	\N	\N	81c2560c-576c-4ac3-98b9-8e79a4843e3e	f574a2d9-40d9-49d0-86dd-c0f1866ad7ee	2026-02-04 02:49:54.917065+00	2026-02-04 02:52:43.363314+00	f	approved	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
910dfec0-2da4-4646-a664-9271a8ad2a15	calvados	Calvados	Brandy	Fruit	48.880000	0.170000	Normandy	France	14th century	1553	\N	Calvados is mostly made from apples and must be made in certain areas  of Normandy, France and aged in French Oak barrels for at least two years.	\N	\N	https://www.foodandwine.com/calvados-brandy-guide-8638558	\N	\N	81c2560c-576c-4ac3-98b9-8e79a4843e3e	81c2560c-576c-4ac3-98b9-8e79a4843e3e	2026-02-04 02:55:24.664359+00	2026-02-04 02:55:24.664359+00	f	approved	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
